Package: Pando
Version: 1.6.1
Type: Package
Title: Inferring regulomes from multi-modal single-cell genomics data
Authors@R: person(given = "Jonas Simon",
               family = "Fleck",
               role = c("aut", "cre"),
               email = "jonas.fleck@bsse.ethz.ch",
               comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-4686-7254"))
Maintainer: Jonas Simon Fleck <jonas.fleck@bsse.ethz.ch>
Description: Pando jointly uses scRNA-seq and scATAC-seq measurements to infer
    regulatory relationships between TF-binding site pairs and target genes.
    infer_condition_grn automatically uses the original infer_grn workflow when
    no condition column is supplied, the requested column is absent, or fewer
    than two condition levels are observed; that route creates no condition
    coefficients. With two or more conditions, one exact TF-peak-target
    candidate supergraph and an equal-condition within-condition variance scale
    are used within each broad cell type. For each target, R constructs only the
    candidate sparse TF-by-ATAC design and deterministic condition-stratified
    fold labels. One registered C++17/RcppEigen target engine then performs the
    complete outer/inner nested cross-validation, fold-local transforms,
    automatic or supplied lambda paths, sparse-group warm-start fitting,
    support-constrained direct-Schur refits, held-out prediction and projection,
    final full-data lambda selection, final refit, and OOF validation. The
    engine reuses fold-local sufficient statistics and batches every inner-fold
    refit path without returning through the R interpreter. The TF-by-ATAC
    product, sparse-group solver, refit kernel and fused target engine are all
    C++-only; missing registration, invalid dimensions, failed Cholesky systems,
    non-finite arithmetic, incomplete OOF assignment or residual-check failures
    terminate the analysis. The primary projection is condition-full
    outer-heldout support: jointly estimable edges form the common component,
    condition-specific estimable edges remain in the focal condition, and every
    non-estimable edge contributes a projectable structural zero in that
    condition. Exact-zero predictors remain represented in the shared candidate
    supergraph without being assigned fitted coefficients.
